WB.L("incentives", "money", "beginner", "Incentives: why rewards so often backfire",
  "Offer people a reward and they'll respond, just not always the way you hoped. Here's why clever incentives can go spectacularly wrong.",
  [
    ["The great rat hunt", "People respond to what is actually rewarded, not what the designer meant.",
      "In 1902 French colonial authorities in Hanoi faced rats in the city's new sewers, and plague made it urgent. They offered a small bounty for every rat killed, paid on presentation of a tail. Soon officials noticed rats running around without tails. People were cutting off tails for the reward and releasing the rats to breed. The historian Michael Vann found reports of rat farming too.\n\nThe scheme did what it paid for, not what it meant. People respond to what is actually rewarded, not what the designer intended. It's one of the most important and most forgotten lessons in economics."],
    ["People respond", "An incentive is anything that makes an action more or less attractive, and careless design invites gaming.",
      "If there's one sentence many economists would put on a T shirt, it's 'people respond to incentives'. An incentive is anything that makes an action more or less attractive: a price, a bonus, a fine, a deadline, even praise or embarrassment. Raise the price of something and people tend to buy less of it.\n\nMany responses are what policymakers want. Australia's container deposit schemes and rooftop solar incentives have changed behaviour on a large scale. The trouble comes when incentives are designed carelessly, because people are quick to find the easiest path to a reward, whether or not it achieves the actual goal."],
    ["Measures as targets", "When a measure becomes a target, people game it and it stops being a good measure.",
      "In 1975 the economist Charles Goodhart observed that statistics used to guide policy tend to break down once they become targets. Marilyn Strathern later summed it up: 'When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.'\n\nA call centre rewarding short call times may find staff hanging up on difficult customers. A hospital judged on waiting times may find creative ways to reclassify waits. Schools judged on test scores can end up teaching to the test. There's an old Soviet era joke about a nail factory: given a target by weight, it made a few giant useless nails; given a target by number, it made millions of tiny ones. Whatever gets measured gets gamed."],
    ["Fines and crowding out", "A small fine can turn a moral duty into a price, and money can crowd out other motives.",
      "In a 2000 study, Uri Gneezy and Aldo Rustichini looked at Israeli day care centres where parents were often late. Some introduced a small fine, and lateness rose. The paper's title, 'A Fine Is a Price', gives the reason: late pick-up had meant imposing on a teacher's goodwill, but now it felt like a service parents could buy. When the fines were removed, lateness stayed high. A cheap financial incentive had replaced a social one, and the old norm didn't return.\n\nMoney can crowd out other motives. In 1970 Richard Titmuss argued that paying for blood could undermine the civic duty of voluntary donors. Researchers debate how strong this is, and payment raises effort in many settings. But people are also motivated by pride, fairness and belonging. Australia's Lifeblood relies on unpaid volunteers."],
    ["Moral hazard and design", "Good incentive design rewards the true goal, expects gaming, and remembers that trust and pride are incentives too.",
      "Moral hazard is an insurance term: if someone else bears the cost when things go wrong, people may be less careful. A driver with comprehensive insurance might park carelessly. A bank expecting a rescue might make riskier loans, keeping profits and passing losses to taxpayers, a worry after 2008. Insurers use excesses so customers share part of every claim. It can be managed, not eliminated.\n\nAustralia's banking Royal Commission, which reported in 2019, heard many examples of staff chasing commissions and sales targets at customers' expense. To design better incentives, reward the true goal rather than an easy proxy, imagine how a cynical person might game it and close those loopholes, and use several measures. Pride, trust and fairness are incentives too. Ask the rat tail question: what am I really paying for?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What went wrong with the Hanoi rat bounty?", ["Nobody wanted to hunt rats for the money","Officials refused to pay for the tails","People bred rats and cut off tails to collect rewards","The bounty was too large for the city to afford"], 2, "The reward was for tails, so people found cheaper ways to produce tails than to reduce rats."],
    [1, "What does 'people respond to incentives' mean?", ["People always do exactly what designers hope","Only cash rewards ever change behaviour","Incentives work only on businesses and not individuals","Prices, rewards and penalties change behaviour, sometimes in unintended ways"], 3, "People take the easiest path to a reward, whether or not it serves the real goal."],
    [2, "What does Goodhart's law warn about?", ["Statistics can never be collected accurately","A measure used as a target tends to stop being a good measure","Targets always improve the quality of services","Large organisations should avoid all measurement"], 1, "Once people are judged on a number, they work on the number rather than the goal behind it."],
    [3, "Why did the day care fine increase lateness?", ["It was larger than parents could afford","Teachers refused to take the fine","Parents did not know about the fine","It made lateness feel like a price parents could pay"], 3, "The fine replaced a social obligation with a cheap financial one, and the norm didn't return."],
    [4, "What is the first step in designing a better incentive?", ["Set a single measure as a strict target","Reward the true goal rather than an easy proxy","Offer the largest possible bonus","Remove all penalties for bad outcomes"], 1, "Rewarding the real goal leaves fewer easy loopholes for people to exploit."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A bounty on rat tails produced more rats. What does that say about rewards?"});

WB.L("great-economic-debate", "money", "advanced", "Smith, Marx, Keynes and Hayek: the great economic debate",
  "Four thinkers shaped how the modern world argues about markets, governments and fairness. Their ideas still echo in every budget and election.",
  [
    ["Smith and specialisation", "Adam Smith argued that specialisation and trade create wealth, with self-interest kept in check by competition and rules.",
      "In 1776 Adam Smith opened The Wealth of Nations with a pin factory. One worker alone might make one pin a day, and certainly not twenty. Ten workers splitting the job into specialised steps could make around 48,000. Specialisation plus trade, he argued, was the engine of prosperity.\n\nA butcher or baker feeds us out of self-interest, not kindness, and competition steers that self-interest towards serving others. He used the phrase 'invisible hand' only once in the book. Smith was no cheerleader for business: he distrusted merchants conspiring to raise prices, criticised monopolies, and backed public spending on defence, justice, infrastructure and some basic education. He had also written The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759), on how sympathy underpins morality."],
    ["Marx on capitalism", "Karl Marx argued that owners profit from the value workers create beyond their wages, and that capitalism is prone to crisis.",
      "Nearly a century later, Karl Marx looked at the same industrial world and saw crowded slums, long hours and child labour. In The Communist Manifesto (1848, with Friedrich Engels) and the first volume of Capital (1867), he argued that capitalism rested on conflict between those who owned factories and those who worked in them.\n\nWorkers, he claimed, produced more value than they were paid, and owners kept the difference as profit. He predicted repeated crises and eventual overthrow. His ideas inspired socialist movements and later communist states, whose record of repression and economic failure has been heavily criticised. Many historians still credit him with sharpening questions about power, inequality and economic change."],
    ["Keynes and demand", "Keynes argued that in a slump, governments should spend more or cut taxes to restart demand, even if that means borrowing.",
      "In the Great Depression of the 1930s, mass unemployment lasted for years, and advice to wait for markets to correct themselves looked hopeless. In The General Theory (1936), John Maynard Keynes offered a different diagnosis: an economy can get stuck with too little total spending, or demand.\n\nWhen households and businesses are frightened, they cut back. That cuts other people's incomes and causes more cutbacks. In such times, he said, governments should step in, spending more or cutting taxes to restart demand, even if it means borrowing. He had earlier written the much quoted line 'In the long run we are all dead', a jab at those who said things would sort themselves out eventually."],
    ["Hayek and prices", "Friedrich Hayek argued that prices carry scattered knowledge that no central planner could ever gather.",
      "Keynes's great rival was the Austrian-born economist Friedrich Hayek. In The Road to Serfdom (1944), he warned that heavy government planning could gradually erode personal freedom. His most influential idea came in a 1945 essay, 'The Use of Knowledge in Society'.\n\nUseful knowledge, Hayek pointed out, is scattered among millions of people. A farmer knows the weather on her land, and a shopkeeper knows what customers want this week. No central planner could gather it all. Prices act like a vast communication system. If tin becomes scarce, its price rises, and people everywhere economise on tin without needing to know why. Markets, in this view, are a way of coordinating knowledge."],
    ["Swings and balance", "Each thinker saw something real, and today's mixed economies argue mainly about the balance between markets and government.",
      "After the Second World War, Keynesian ideas shaped policy across much of the West, including Australia, with governments aiming for full employment. The 1970s changed that. Oil shocks helped trigger stagflation, high inflation combined with high unemployment, which many Keynesian models struggled to explain. Milton Friedman, who argued that 'inflation is always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon', gained influence.\n\nFrom the 1980s, many governments, Australia's included, floated currencies, deregulated finance and gave central banks the job of controlling inflation. After 2008, Keynesian stimulus returned. Today almost every developed economy is a mixed economy, and most political arguments are about the balance: how much to tax, spend and regulate, and how to share the gains from growth."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Smith's pin factory example show?", ["Splitting a job into specialised steps multiplies what workers can produce","Small workshops always beat large factories","Merchants should set prices without competition","Workers do their best work when they act alone"], 0, "Specialisation and trade, Smith argued, are the engine of prosperity."],
    [1, "According to Marx, where did owners' profits come from?", ["Clever investment in gold","The value workers produced beyond what they were paid","Government subsidies to industry","Trade with distant colonies"], 1, "Marx argued owners kept the gap between the value workers created and their wages as profit."],
    [2, "Why did Keynes want governments to spend during a slump?", ["Frightened households cut back, which cuts incomes and demand further","Markets always correct themselves within a year","Government debt is always harmless","Higher taxes encourage people to save"], 0, "Falling demand can feed on itself, so Keynes argued governments should restart it."],
    [3, "What did Hayek say prices do?", ["They reveal which products are morally valuable","They carry scattered knowledge to people who need it","They stay fixed unless planners change them","They only reflect what producers spend"], 1, "Prices coordinate the knowledge of millions of people, which no planner could gather."],
    [4, "How are most developed economies, including Australia's, best described?", ["Fully planned by government","Entirely free of regulation","Mixed, combining private markets with public services and regulation","Based on gold rather than currency"], 2, "Most political argument is about the balance within a mixed economy, not about choosing one extreme."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Four thinkers, one pin factory, and an argument still shaping every budget."});

